Peggy: I am leaning towards **"How do economic conditions influence housing insecurity?"** The authors repeatedly emphasized economic conditions, introduced two economic hypotheses, and examined whether changes in those conditions were associated with changes in homelessness.

Ty: Agreed! I can see why some of the other choices might sound right—they use ideas that appear in the study. But they either focus on just one piece of the study or ask about something the researchers weren’t actually investigating. This question brings together the economic conditions they examined with the homelessness outcome they were trying to explain.

Lianne: Great! I went ahead and added **"How do economic conditions influence housing insecurity?"** to our table.

::hs_econ_table2 [display=target:hs_main label="Comparison table"]

Ty: I bet we can locate clues for Evidence Used and Methods in the Method section of the source text. Why don’t we each read that section and highlight clues?

Peggy: Sounds good to me. Evidence used refers to data they collected and Methods is how they collected that data. Right?

Lianne: Yes, that is right. Go ahead and highlight text for Evidence Used and Methods.

::hs_econ_em_hl_screen [display=target:hs_main label="Evidence and methods"]

